  "source": "TS Lombard",
  "title": "Putting Money Back Into Monetary Policy",
  "type": "macro",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-09-24",
  "insights": [
   "A 'monetarist revival' is underway in central banking, led by new Fed Chair Kevin Warsh, who advocates for reincorporating monetary aggregates into policy frameworks.",
   "While monetary data provided an early warning for the post-COVID inflation surge, the report argues that this revival will have limited practical impact on financial markets or policy outcomes.",
   "The report is skeptical that tracking money supply will lead to better policy, noting that the relationship between money and inflation is unstable in the short term due to shifts in velocity.",
   "Central banks are expected to pay 'lip service' to monetary aggregates, similar to the late 1990s, without letting them dictate short-term interest rate decisions.",
   "Investors should not take the threat of aggressive Quantitative Tightening (QT) too seriously, as there is no current FOMC consensus for it and it risks financial stability.",
   "If the Fed does pursue QT, it will likely be accompanied by bank deregulation, which would be supportive of risk assets.",
   "The report highlights that monetarist forecasts for a 2023 recession were incorrect, demonstrating that monetary analysis is not a panacea for macro forecasting."
